cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this code, the synthesized stimulus set, or the waveform_similarity()
  workflow, please cite this archive. Once the accompanying paper is published,
  please cite the paper as well.
title: >-
  Code and data for "Waveform-based acoustic analysis is sensitive to acoustic
  fine structure to a similar degree as birds, while traditional techniques are not"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: McLean
    given-names: Celia R.
    affiliation: Department of Psychology, Cornell University, Ithaca NY, USA
  - family-names: Odom
    given-names: Karan J.
    affiliation: >-
      Department of Biological Sciences, University of the Pacific,
      Stockton CA, USA
  - family-names: Araya-Salas
    given-names: Marcelo
    affiliation: >-
      Centro de Investigacion en Neurociencias & Escuela de Biologia,
      Universidad de Costa Rica, San Jose, Costa Rica
    email: marcelo.araya@ucr.ac.cr
  - family-names: Prior
    given-names: Nora H.
    affiliation: Department of Psychology, Cornell University, Ithaca NY, USA
version: 1.0.0
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23677232
date-released: '2026-10-26'
license: GPL-2.0-or-later
repository-code: https://github.com/maRce10/acoustic-fine-structure-methods
url: https://marce10.github.io/acoustic-fine-structure-methods/
abstract: >-
  Code, synthesized Schroeder-phase stimuli and analysis reports accompanying a
  comparison of nine acoustic structure quantification methods for their ability
  to detect acoustic fine structure phase differences, and an evaluation of two
  waveform-based methods under decreasing signal-to-noise ratios.
keywords:
  - bioacoustics
  - acoustic fine structure
  - temporal fine structure
  - Schroeder-phase harmonic complexes
  - waveform correlation
  - dynamic time warping
  - birdsong
  - warbleR
